WEBVTT

00:04.638 --> 00:07.350
Most shots go off as planned. Some don't.

00:07.350 --> 00:10.013
A misfire is a complete or partial failure of the

00:10.013 --> 00:12.963
explosive to detonate as planned. That can be a

00:12.963 --> 00:15.975
whole round that never fires, or a single hole

00:15.975 --> 00:18.725
in the round that doesn't. A misfire is dangerous

00:18.725 --> 00:21.625
because it's still explosive, and it can be disturbed

00:21.625 --> 00:24.300
by something as ordinary as a loader bucket,

00:24.300 --> 00:26.012
a drill, or a boot.

00:27.145 --> 00:29.982
That's why MSHA has specific rules for what happens

00:29.982 --> 00:33.020
after a suspected misfire, and why your only job

00:33.020 --> 00:34.932
is to stay away and report.

00:36.054 --> 00:38.179
What does one look like? You might see a length

00:38.179 --> 00:40.904
of wire or cord coming out of the muck or out of

00:40.904 --> 00:43.416
the rock. You might see a hole that didn't break

00:43.416 --> 00:45.604
the way the others did. You might see something

00:45.604 --> 00:48.604
that looks like a cartridge or a piece of explosive.

00:48.604 --> 00:51.367
You are not expected to identify what it is.

00:51.367 --> 00:53.392
If something looks wrong after a shot,

00:53.392 --> 00:55.079
treat it as a misfire.

00:58.408 --> 01:01.583
Your job when you find a suspected misfire is short.

01:01.583 --> 01:04.208
Don't touch it. Don't move it. Don't dig around

01:04.208 --> 01:07.395
it, load it, or haul it away. Back away the way

01:07.395 --> 01:10.645
you came, and keep anyone else from going closer.

01:10.645 --> 01:13.295
Then tell the blaster in charge or your supervisor

01:13.295 --> 01:16.033
right away, by radio from a safe distance,

01:16.033 --> 01:18.033
and tell them exactly where it is.

01:19.162 --> 01:21.587
Never assume somebody else has already seen it.

01:21.587 --> 01:23.862
Never let someone talk you into just nudging it

01:23.862 --> 01:26.687
clear. And never try to deal with it yourself.

01:27.832 --> 01:29.933
Because you can't tell what's in the rock.

01:29.933 --> 01:33.082
A bucket cutting into a muck pile, a drill steel

01:33.082 --> 01:36.657
hitting an old hole, a bar prying at a socket:

01:36.657 --> 01:38.870
any of those can set off explosive that didn't

01:38.870 --> 01:41.733
fire the first time. Anything that looks harmless

01:41.733 --> 01:43.433
could be the one that isn't.

01:44.566 --> 01:47.716
Watch out for three excuses. "It's probably nothing."

01:47.716 --> 01:50.428
Maybe, but let the blaster decide that.

01:50.428 --> 01:53.328
"We're behind schedule." No schedule is worth it.

01:53.328 --> 01:55.578
And "I'll just mark it for them." Don't go in to

01:55.578 --> 01:58.328
mark it. Tell the blaster where it is from a safe

01:58.328 --> 02:01.603
distance, and let them handle it. If anyone pressures

02:01.603 --> 02:04.703
you to ignore a suspected misfire, that's exactly

02:04.703 --> 02:07.528
the moment to use the stop-work right from Module

02:07.528 --> 02:08.391
12.

02:11.720 --> 02:14.807
Once a misfire is suspected, MSHA's rules set a

02:14.807 --> 02:17.595
waiting period before anyone goes near it.

02:17.595 --> 02:19.320
Depending on the type of detonator,

02:19.320 --> 02:21.832
the rule is fifteen or thirty minutes,

02:21.832 --> 02:24.320
or longer if the manufacturer says so.

02:24.320 --> 02:26.707
The blaster in charge follows it, and your site's

02:26.707 --> 02:28.795
procedure tells you who does what.

02:29.906 --> 02:32.656
After the wait, only the work needed to dispose

02:32.656 --> 02:35.406
of the misfire is allowed in the affected area,

02:35.406 --> 02:37.731
and only by people who need to be there.

02:37.731 --> 02:40.456
If it can't be disposed of safely, every approach

02:40.456 --> 02:43.006
to the area has to be posted with a warning sign

02:43.006 --> 02:45.368
that keeps people out, and it's reported to mine

02:45.368 --> 02:48.293
management right away. Any misfire during a shift

02:48.293 --> 02:50.518
gets reported to management by the end of that

02:50.518 --> 02:51.406
shift.

02:54.744 --> 02:57.144
The same danger shows up when you drill.

02:57.144 --> 02:59.894
A bootleg is what's left of a drilled hole after

02:59.894 --> 03:03.207
a shot, a socket in the rock. Because a bootleg

03:03.207 --> 03:05.782
or a missed hole can still hold explosive,

03:05.782 --> 03:08.394
the drilling rules are strict. No drilling where

03:08.394 --> 03:10.894
there's a danger of hitting a hole that's charged

03:10.894 --> 03:13.757
or misfired. And no starting a new hole inside

03:13.757 --> 03:14.682
a bootleg.

03:15.810 --> 03:18.423
This applies on surface benches and underground

03:18.423 --> 03:20.810
in the headers. If you're on the drill crew,

03:20.810 --> 03:22.760
you already know the drill marks come from the

03:22.760 --> 03:25.098
blaster's pattern, and you don't move them on your

03:25.098 --> 03:28.010
own. If you're anyone else, and you see a socket

03:28.010 --> 03:30.223
or a hole where the drill is about to go,

03:30.223 --> 03:33.460
stop the drill and tell the driller or supervisor.

03:36.796 --> 03:38.846
Here's how it should sound. You're cleaning up

03:38.846 --> 03:41.296
near the face after the all-clear, and you spot

03:41.296 --> 03:43.496
a bit of wire sticking out of the muck.

03:43.496 --> 03:46.546
You stop. You back away, and you wave your coworker

03:46.546 --> 03:49.109
off. You radio the blaster in charge from where

03:49.109 --> 03:51.446
you're standing. You say where it is and what you

03:51.446 --> 03:54.146
saw. And you wait where you can see the area,

03:54.146 --> 03:56.871
so that nobody walks in. That's the whole job,

03:56.871 --> 03:58.346
and it might save a life.

03:59.470 --> 04:02.720
To sum up. After a shot, treat anything odd as

04:02.720 --> 04:05.595
a misfire. Don't touch it, don't move it,

04:05.595 --> 04:07.920
keep others out, and tell the blaster in charge

04:07.920 --> 04:10.658
right away. The blaster follows the waiting period

04:10.658 --> 04:13.483
and handles it. And nobody ever drills in,

04:13.483 --> 04:15.133
or into, an old hole.

04:16.252 --> 04:19.227
Your site's plans always override this video.
